tail/20872.html","ph":"099-219-9488","wifi":1},{"n":"Kagoshima Bank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":31.592218,"lo":130.556422,"d":849},{"n":"UFJ銀行","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":31.590624,"lo":130.555837,"d":870},{"n":"Diocese of Kagoshima","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.591667,"lo":130.550556,"d":1380,"ds":"The Diocese of Kagoshima (Latin: Dioecesis Kagoshimaensis, Japanese: カトリック鹿児島教区) is a Latin Church diocese of the Catholic Church in the ecclesiastical province of the Metropolitan Archbishop of Nagasaki 長崎, in southern Japan. Its episcopal see is the Cathedral of St. Francis Xavier (Xavier Church), in the city of Kagoshima.","wpu":"Diocese of Kagoshima","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/St_Francis_Xavier_Cathedral_%28Terukunich%C5%8D%2C_Kagoshima%29_VLux5_hdsr_05.jpg/960px-St_Francis_Xavier_Cathedral_%28Terukunich%C5%8D%2C_Kagoshima%29_VLux5_hdsr_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Terukuni shrine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.594806,"lo":130.550083,"d":1510,"ds":"Terukuni jinja (照国神社) is a Shinto shrine in the city of Kagoshima in Kagoshima Prefecture in Japan. This shrine is considered to be a dwelling place for the kami of Shimazu Nariakira, whose posthumous name is Terukuni Daimyōjin (照国大明神).","wpu":"Terukuni shrine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Haiden_of_Terukuni_Shrine.JPG/960px-Haiden_of_Terukuni_Shrine.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ishibashi Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.604333,"lo":130.568528,"d":1628,"ds":"Ishibashi Park (石橋記念公園, Ishibashi Kinen Kōen) is a park in Hama-machi, Kagoshima, Japan. At the end of the Edo period (late 19th century), local lord Shimazu Shigehide had five bridges, collectively called the Gosekkyō (\"five stone bridges\"), built across the Kōtsuki River. Two of them collapsed in floods in 1993.","wpu":"Ishibashi Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Bridge_in_Kagoshima.JPG/960px-Bridge_in_Kagoshima.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museum of the Meiji Restoration","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.584639,"lo":130.54775,"d":1739,"ds":"The Museum of the Meiji Restoration (維新ふるさと館, Ishin-furusato-kan) is a history museum in Kagoshima, Japan. Located by the Kōtsuki River, it is a gallery where visitors can learn about the Meiji Restoration. In the basement hall, sound, light, and robots are used to present a three-dimensional experience of the Meiji Restoration.","wpu":"Museum of the Meiji Restoration","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Museum_of_the_Meiji_Restoration_01.jpg/960px-Museum_of_the_Meiji_Restoration_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Amuran","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.584722,"lo":130.5425,"d":2210,"ds":"Amuran (アミュラン, Amyuran) is a Ferris wheel in Kagoshima, Japan. The wheel sits on top of Amu Plaza Kagoshima adjoining Kagoshima-Chūō Station, and is one of the major attractions in the city. The wheel has a diameter of 60 m, and is 91 m from the ground at its highest point.","wpu":"Amuran","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/Amu1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Kagoshima Spinning Mill","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.615556,"lo":130.574167,"d":2971,"ds":"The Kagoshima Spinning Mill (鹿児島紡績所跡, Kagoshima bōsekijo ato) is a former cotton spinning mill constructed in the Bakumatsu period by Satsuma Domain in the Yoshino neighborhood of the city of Kagoshima Japan.","wpu":"Kagoshima Spinning Mill","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Former_Kagoshima_Spinning_Engineer%27s_Residence_-_2.jpg/960px-Former_Kagoshima_Spinning_Engineer%27s_Residence_-_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nagashima Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.581771,"lo":130.532387,"d":3221,"ds":"Nagashima Museum (長島美術館, Nagashima bijutsukan) opened in Kagoshima, Kagoshima Prefecture, Japan, in 1989, the centenary of the city's official foundation. Located on a hill overlooking Sakurajima and Kagoshima Bay, at an elevation of 110 metres, the museum's collection of some thousand objects amassed by businessman Nagashima Kōsuke (長島公佑), includes works by Kuroda Seiki, Rodin, and Chagall, as well as Satsuma ware.","wpu":"Nagashima Museum","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Female_Portrait_by_Fujishima_Takeji_%28Nagashima_Museum%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Sengan-en","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.618333,"lo":130.579444,"d":3434,"ds":"Sengan-en (仙巌園) is a Japanese garden attached to a former Shimazu clan residence in Kagoshima, Kagoshima Prefecture, Japan. Designated a Place of Scenic Beauty, together with the adjacent Shōko Shūseikan it forms part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site Sites of Japan’s Meiji Industrial Revolution: Iron and Steel, Shipbuilding and Coal Mining. Sengan-en is managed by Shimadzu Ltd.","wpu":"Sengan-en","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sengan-en2.jpg/960px-Sengan-en2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sakurajima","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":31.580556,"lo":130.658056,"d":8876,"ds":"Sakurajima (Japanese: 桜島, lit. 'Cherry Blossom Island') is an active stratovolcano, formerly an island and now a peninsula, in Kagoshima Prefecture in Kyushu, Japan. It is the most active volcano in Japan. As of November 2025, the volcanic activity continues, dropping volcanic ash on the surroundings.","wpu":"Sakurajima","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Sakurajima55.jpg/960px-Sakurajima55.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Kagoshima , is the capital city of Kagoshima Prefecture, Japan. As of 31 July 2024, the city had an estimated population of 583,966 in 285,992 households, and a population density of 1100 persons per km2. The total area of the city is 547.61 km2 (211.43 sq mi).","wpu":"Kagoshima","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Kagoshima_Montage.jpg/960px-Kagoshima_Montage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}